---
import LegalDocument from '@/components/legal/legal-document.astro';
import PageIntro from '@/components/page-intro.astro';
import SubpageLayout from '@/layouts/subpage-layout.astro';

const toc = [
  { id: 'using-groam', title: 'Using Groam' },
  { id: 'your-account', title: 'Your account' },
  { id: 'your-content', title: 'Your content' },
  { id: 'groups', title: 'Groups and shared trips' },
  { id: 'ai', title: 'AI agents' },
  { id: 'travel', title: 'Travel and bookings' },
  { id: 'pricing', title: 'Pricing' },
  { id: 'acceptable-use', title: 'Acceptable use' },
  { id: 'availability', title: 'Availability' },
  { id: 'ending', title: 'Ending use' },
  { id: 'changes', title: 'Changes to these terms' }
];
---

<SubpageLayout
  title="Terms"
  description="The agreement between you and Groam when you use it to plan trips."
>
  <PageIntro
    title="Terms"
    lede="The agreement between you and Groam when you use it to plan trips with your group."
  />
  <LegalDocument
    draftNote="These terms are not ready to accept yet. The contracting entity, contact details, governing law, age rules, payment and refund terms, suspension process, and liability terms still need owner and legal review."
    toc={toc}
    updated="18 October 2026"
  >
    <section id="using-groam">
      <h2>Using Groam</h2>
      <p>
        Groam is a place to plan trips with a group: a shared itinerary, ideas, issues, and
        conversations. These terms are a working draft and do not yet identify the party offering
        the service, so they should not be presented as the final agreement at sign-up.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section id="your-account">
      <h2>Your account</h2>
      <p>
        Give accurate account information, keep your sign-in details safe, and do not share an
        account. You are responsible for activity you authorize through your account. The final
        terms need a real contact route for reporting suspected unauthorized access.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section id="your-content">
      <h2>Your content</h2>
      <p>
        You keep any rights you have in trips, ideas, issues, messages, and files you add. You give
        the service operator permission to host, copy, process, and display that content only as
        needed to operate the features you choose, share it with the relevant group, protect the
        service, and follow the law. You confirm that you have the rights and permissions needed to
        add and share that content.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section id="groups">
      <h2>Groups and shared trips</h2>
      <p>
        Trips live in groups. Group members can access shared trip content when no payment is
        required or their payment is confirmed, even if they are not going. Unpaid members of
        a billable trip see an invitation preview until payment is confirmed; they can pay
        themselves or an organizer can pay for them. The trip creator is free. Members with
        access can suggest changes and discuss trips, while organizers have additional
        controls. Activity may be recorded with its author. Group roles and access can change,
        so review the group roster and think about what you share.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section id="ai">
      <h2>AI agents</h2>
      <p>
        AI features use your connected ChatGPT access and may send your prompt and relevant trip
        context to OpenAI. OpenAI’s terms also apply to that connection. AI output can be wrong,
        incomplete, out of date, or unsuitable. Review an agent’s sources and proposed changes
        before applying them. Do not rely on AI output as professional, safety, immigration,
        medical, legal, or financial advice.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section id="travel">
      <h2>Travel and bookings</h2>
      <p>
        Groam helps a group organize information. It is not a travel agency and does not make or
        guarantee bookings. Prices, opening hours, schedules, availability, entry rules, visa and
        health requirements, and safety conditions can change. Check important details with the
        airline, hotel, venue, public authority, or other provider before paying or travelling.
        Your contracts with those providers are separate from your use of Groam.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section id="pricing">
      <h2>Pricing</h2>
      <p>
        Planning on your own is free. When payments are enabled, invited travelers pay €1 per trip through Stripe Checkout, or a trip organizer covers their payment. The trip creator is free. Existing trips stay free, and payments are skipped when Stripe is not configured. The final terms still need owner and legal review of when a charge is due, who takes payment, taxes, receipts, failed payments, cancellation and refund rules, and any consumer withdrawal rights.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section id="acceptable-use">
      <h2>Acceptable use</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Do not break the law or use Groam to harm other people.</li>
        <li>Do not try to get into accounts, trips, or systems that are not yours.</li>
        <li>Do not disrupt the service or misuse it at a scale that affects others.</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section id="availability">
      <h2>Availability</h2>
      <p>
        Groam may change, pause, or experience errors. Keep copies of information you need while
        travelling. This draft deliberately does not set liability exclusions or caps: those terms
        require the operator’s identity, governing law, consumer context, and legal review, and
        nothing here limits rights that cannot legally be limited.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section id="ending">
      <h2>Ending use</h2>
      <p>
        You can stop using Groam and leave groups using the controls the product provides. Trips can
        currently be archived rather than deleted. The final terms must explain account closure,
        content export and deletion, when access may be suspended, notice and appeal, and what terms
        continue after an account closes.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section id="changes">
      <h2>Changes to these terms</h2>
      <p>
        The date at the top will change when this draft changes. The final terms must explain how
        material changes will be communicated and when they take effect. Continued use alone will
        not be treated as acceptance where the law or the nature of the change requires a clearer
        form of agreement.
      </p>
    </section>
  </LegalDocument>
</SubpageLayout>
